Revision history for Selecto-Components

Unreleased
    - Leave out rows with no value, on a group bucketed by ranges, now keeps
      only values inside a range: the Other bucket (missing and out of range)
      goes, from rows, columns and totals.
    - A pivot shows every range of a bucketed column group as a column, in the
      order given, even when nothing falls in it.
    - The summary pivot names the group shown as columns in a header row
      above its values.
    - Show Total columns: a checkbox beside Show as columns, on by default.
      pivot_totals=0 leaves the Total column group out of the summary pivot
      and the grid pivot, their exports and their queries; the Total row and
      the subtotal rows stay. The parameter is left out when on, so existing
      links render as before. max_grid_result_cells counts only the cells
      rendered. A grid with no group shown as columns keeps its Total column.
    - Show as columns in the summary table. A Group By marked Show as columns
      now pivots the paged aggregate table as well as the grid: the other
      groups stay rows with their rollup subtotals, order, pages and
      drilldowns, each column value heads one sub-column per measure, and a
      Total group holds each row's totals. Every page has the same columns
      and whole-result totals: a page's rows come from the ordinary summary
      query, and its cells from one grouped query per rollup level shown,
      plus one for the column values. Values and column headers drill to
      Detail on the row values and the column value. Explorer->export
      flattens the header and keeps subtotal and total rows. The parameter is
      now pivot_column; grid_column is still read. max_grid_columns and
      max_grid_result_cells bound it. A summary without the parameter, the
      grid and the graph render as before.
    - Leave out rows with no value: a checkbox on each Group By
      (group_exclude_missing=<group field>) that filters out rows where the
      group's field is null, in every aggregate layout. Totals leave them
      out too. It is listed with the filters as coming from the group.
    - Aggregate grid: one Group By can be marked "Show as columns"
      (grid_column=<group field>), and the grid then takes any number of row
      groups and measures. Each column value heads one sub-column per
      measure; a Total column group and a Total row hold the row, column and
      grand totals, fetched with grouping sets (cube for two groups). Columns
      of bucketed groups keep the order their ranges were given in. Colour
      is scaled per measure. Cell, row and column selection filter on the row
      values and the column value; grid_cell carries one value per group and
      grid_axis may name several row groups ({axes, values}). Explorer->export
      of a pivot model flattens the header to "<column value> · <measure>".
      New limit max_grid_columns (default 50), and max_grid_result_cells
      counts a pivot's rendered cells (rows x columns x measures, with
      totals). A grid of two groups and one measure without grid_column
      renders and is limited exactly as before. Not included: subtotals per
      row-group prefix, a pivot in Selecto::API::Explorer's complete grid,
      and web export of grids, which is refused as before.
    - A measure's graph series settings (style, Y axis, stack group, colour,
      fill opacity) show only in the Graph view. They stay in the form in the
      other views, so the settings survive a change of view.
    - API Console: an Aggregates query profile on hosts that enable
      canonical_explorer. It builds grouped aggregate queries from the
      explorer features document, with rollup, cube and grouping-set
      subtotals, and marks subtotal and total rows in the result table.
    - The aggregate grid shows a Total column, a Total row and a grand total
      on adapters with the cube capability. The grid query then groups with
      CUBE instead of ROLLUP; the totals are the database's aggregates, not
      sums of the visible cells. Needs a selecto-perl with group_by_cube.
    - canonical_explorer (explorer host options) adds remote_query.explorer.v1
      to the canonical base: GET explorer-features and POST explorer-query over
      Selecto::API::Explorer, advertised in the manifest, with the query
      route's X-CSRF-Token and same-origin rules on the POST.
    - WebSocket runs keep their field catalogs. A message's request copy of the
      configuration started with an empty catalog cache, so every run rebuilt
      and relocalized the catalogs (80 ms a run on lhf Load Detail). The
      socket's ExplorerSession now keeps them for later messages: keyed by the
      domain's fingerprint, dropped when the scope changes or the socket
      closes, and for at most websocket_session_options catalog_ttl seconds
      (default 300). A run on lhf Load Detail: 231 ms to 173 ms.
    - Action choices load with their dialog. An action shown in a dialog whose
      inputs take choices from a choice_source no longer resolves them for
      every page: Actions->available(..., defer_choices => 1), as the Explorer
      asks, marks it form_on_open, and its dialog loads the form from
      PATH/actions/ID/form when it opens (for the rows it was opened on,
      checked as the action itself is). On lhf Load the Add Event choices cost
      every page 120 ms.
    - Detail pages count after they show. A page with more rows after it no
      longer waits for its count: it returns with "N rows on this page ·
      counting all matches…" and the browser asks the new count route
      (PATH/count, with the page's query; Selecto::Components::Explorer->count)
      for the total and pagination, which replace the placeholders (a page
      replaced meanwhile keeps what it has). Config defer_count, default true;
      with query_params off the page counts first.
    - A count query runs directly, without a cursor: it returns one row, so
      Selecto::BoundedQuery takes its direct path (savepoint with timeout,
      query, rollback). Five round trips instead of thirteen, and PostgreSQL no
      longer plans it for a cursor's first rows (lhf Load Detail: 1160 ms to
      334 ms).
    - Detail pages count only when they must. A Detail page fetches one row
      beyond its size, as aggregate pages do: without that row it ends the
      results and its rows give the total, so no count query runs (Query Debug
      shows "not needed"). A page with more after it, or an empty page past the
      first, is counted as before.
    - Query Debug counts database round trips. With a DBD::Pg handle the panel
      shows how many round trips the Explorer made (data, count and other), the
      time spent waiting on them, the bytes sent and received, and each one with
      what it sent. They are counted from libpq's protocol trace
      (Selecto::Components::RoundTrips), so the BEGIN the driver sends with
      AutoCommit off and the Parse before a second execute are included. Only
      with show_sql; other adapters show a dash.
    - Host header links. Selecto::Components::Config takes header_links_resolver,
      called like api_console_resolver, returning [{label, href}] links shown in the
      Explorer's header actions beside Permalink and API (in private URL mode too).
      Selecto::Components::APIConsole->page takes links, shown in the console
      header (Selecto API Console with data-links). Each href must be an absolute
      same-origin path.
    - The API button works when a detail column is repeated. The canonical API
      refuses two result columns with one name, and the console request copied
      every column. Each repeat, with the same format or another, now gets its own
      alias (created_on_2). Columns given the same configured alias still disable
      the button.
    - A filter added in the browser takes edits made on its promoted card. A
      results-only run re-renders the promoted cards with the server's filter
      instance but keeps the builder, whose new items had none, so the edits were
      dropped. Submitting now numbers builder items as the server does.
    - Field and filter-choice descriptions (Selecto::Domain field_description,
      components.filter_choices description) reach the field catalog, translated as
      fields.<path>.description and components.filter_choices.<path>.description,
      and Explorer shows them: a tooltip on picker choices and column headings, and
      text under the label of chosen fields and filters. Needs selecto-perl with
      field_description.
    - The Query Assistant (WebMCP query form editor) is removed. Move any
      query_assistant.palettes to the explorer option graph_palettes.
    - Resource::Composer and Resource::Registry are removed; nothing used them.
    - Set up the shared API Console in the authenticated PostgreSQL stack
      example, with Explorer query handoff, session CSRF and logout checks.
      Advertise the existing catalog and Explorer metadata routes for current
      Console discovery. Generate Console and Importer cache revisions from
      the packaged asset manifest instead of fixed version strings.
    - A canned page shows a multiple text control as a textarea, so a list
      of values can be pasted one per line.
    - Canned pages: a column_layout entry no longer takes a kind; it follows
      from the entry's keys (field, fields, collection, url_prefix,
      row_number), and kind is refused. The former join is a fields entry.
      A collection entry names the association, and the new
      layout_selections builds a detail query's selections from the layout.
    - Canned layout fields and nested fields take a format (a
      Selecto::DateFormat name, such as the new us_date and us_datetime),
      applied in SQL; layout_selections takes the timezone to show them in.
      The display formats are not offered for Explorer grouping.
    - A canned page with one view and no controls shows no filter panel or
      Apply filters button; the results take the full width.
    - Action prerequisite columns (can_<action>, from Selecto's action
      preconditions and transitions) appear in the Explorer as columns and
      Yes/No filters only while the action authorizer shows their action;
      for a hidden action, both are removed from the request's domain.
    - API console 0.5.11: discovers the canonical getResource route (and still
      the customer API's getCustomerResourceVersion), and chooses between POST
      query and GET resource with radio buttons, shown only when the API
      offers resource GET.
    - An authored query-library segment without parameters may filter on
      internal fields, directly or through a view. Segments that take
      parameters remain refused on internal fields.
    - The Explorer grid, its counts and its CSV/TSV/JSON and XLSX exports ask
      Selecto::PostgreSQL for canonical values, which it no longer returns
      by default, so they are unchanged (2.5, not 2.50; ISO timestamps)
      whatever the host's adapter options. Other adapters ignore the option.
    - Exports check their database deadline with defer_rearm, so the server
      timeout is refreshed before each FETCH rather than once per exported
      row (removes a database round trip per row).
    - Faster exports: CSV/TSV/JSON and XLSX exports fetch 100 rows per
      database round trip instead of one (about 7x faster on a 5,000-row,
      20-column PostgreSQL export, more against a remote database). The
      driver buffers at most 100 rows; per-row cell, row and byte checks are
      unchanged. Needs selecto-perl with fetch_size-aware bounded streams;
      older versions keep fetching one row.
    - 0.1.2 security remediation: bound ordinary Explorer/canned rows, nested
      materialization, response bytes and database work; expire stalled export
      delivery leases and require explicit shared-cache namespaces.
    - Admit action input before callbacks, authorize both choice-range bounds,
      distinguish co-domain lookup authorization, and remove executable SQL
      interpolation. SQL diagnostics require deliberate host debug mode.
    - Require Selecto 0.2.2 for the common bounded result execution boundary.
    - Security: protected WebSockets require live authorization; public and
      disabled modes are explicit. Assistant request bodies, shared draft
      storage, query-state expansion and exports have finite enforced budgets.
    - Security: canonical action targets precede host callbacks; empty choice
      membership is validated; private history and saved URL expansion are
      disabled. See README migration and adapter capability requirements.
    - Graph axes that measure counts tick on whole numbers, so small counts
      no longer repeat rounded labels (3, 3, 2, 2, ...).
    - The canned page record dialog uses the theme's panel colour. It fell
      back to white because the shared theme defines no --sc-surface, which
      left the dark palette's light text unreadable.
    - Non-ASCII values in JSON the explorer and canned pages embed in HTML
      (drilldown buttons, carried drilldown and membership filters, data-*
      attributes, permalinks) and in CSV, TSV and JSON exports are UTF-8
      encoded once instead of twice. Submitted JSON and WebSocket messages
      are parsed as the characters Mojolicious already decoded, so a
      membership filter or drilldown with accented text or emoji selects
      exactly that value instead of failing.
    - Nested to-many columns show non-ASCII values. JSON text from drivers
      that return characters (DBD::Pg on a UTF-8 database, DBD::SQLite with
      sqlite_unicode) failed to parse, and the nested table rendered empty.
    - Optional WebMCP tools edit validated query-form drafts without running
      queries. Drafts support revision checks, idempotent apply, manual-edit
      synchronization, one-step undo, and an optional SQLite store.
    - Development tasks resolve the pinned relocatable Perl directly, including
      commands launched through a shell.
    - WebSocket query runs refresh the permalink and export links to match
      the completed query, including assistant-prepared graph drafts.
    - The explorer retargets its row grain: a detail view whose columns all
      come from one to-many association shows that association's rows, a
      summary that reads one to-many association drills down into the rows
      it counted, and a "Rows of" picker lists the domain's declared
      retarget targets and swaps the pickers to the target's fields. The
      rows_of URL parameter carries the grain. Requires Selecto with
      Query->retarget.
    - Security: the record editor no longer signs or verifies snapshots
      with a guessable key. Without a host-set $app->secrets (Mojolicious
      defaults to the app moniker; the editor fell back to
      'selecto-components') the editor answers 500 and refuses saves.
    - Security: the record-editor signature also binds the session's CSRF
      token, the engine tenant, the domain fingerprint and the time the form
      was opened (record_signed_at). A form replayed in another session or
      tenant is refused, and so is one older than the new
      record_editor_max_age option (default 3600 seconds, at most 86400).
    - Security: the explorer refuses (state error) a query-library segment,
      requested or from a view, that reads an internal or redacted field. A
      components.filter_choices filter over an internal field the host did
      not list in filter_fields, and a conditional choice filter over any
      non-public field, accept only their declared choice values with eq,
      ne, in or not_in; the picker no longer offers them a null test.
      Previously a parameterized segment or a range filter probed the hidden
      values row by row.

0.1.0  2026-09-30
    - First CPAN release: Mojolicious plugin providing the Selecto explorer,
      canned search pages, dashboards, record editor, row and selected-row
      actions, and the API Console over htmx 4 WebSockets with GET/POST
      fallbacks.
    - Native-template pages (Selecto::Components::Templates) moved to the
      separate optional Selecto-Components-Templates add-on.
    - Licensed under the Artistic License 2.0.
    - Strict Content-Security-Policy clean: no inline styles; heat and rollup
      cells use shared theme classes and host theme colours load from a
      validated same-origin stylesheet (Selecto::Components::ThemeStylesheet).
